<section class="block-section story">
  <span class="section-tag">Story</span>
  <h2>What today is about</h2>
  <p class="sub">Unit launch. One question runs through the whole unit: <em>who owns the water?</em> Today is the science underneath it.</p>
  <p>You drew the water cycle in Unit 1 as arrows between spheres. Today you add the part that makes it a political problem: <strong>time</strong>. Water sits in <strong>reservoirs</strong> (the air, soil, rivers, lakes, groundwater, the ocean, snow and ice), and each reservoir has a <strong>residence time</strong>: how long a drop stays there, on average, before it moves on. Air holds a drop for about nine days. The deep ocean holds it for thousands of years.</p>
  <p>That difference is what the WMO report in today's bell ringer is warning about. Fast reservoirs, like rivers, refill in months. Slow reservoirs, like glaciers and groundwater, fill over decades or centuries, and they carry a region through its dry years. Spend them faster than they refill and a single bad monsoon becomes a crisis.</p>
  <p>It is also why some water disputes can be renegotiated and others cannot. A river allocation can be changed next season because the river refills. A shallow aquifer pumped too hard can take years to decades to recover; a deep one, thousands of years. Near the coast, seawater can move into a pumped-out aquifer, and that is much harder to undo. The <strong>Cauvery</strong>, the unit's anchor case, is both at once: an argument over a fast river, sitting on top of groundwater that both Tamil Nadu and Karnataka are drawing down. You meet the full case in Block 6. Today you just find it on your diagram.</p>
  <div class="callout support">
    <div class="callout-title">Not today</div>
    <p>No choice board and no Cauvery reading. One path, in order. You need <strong>paper, a pencil, this page, and your phone for one photo at the end</strong>. The Hydrologist is an extension, not a step.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="block-section concepts">
  <span class="section-tag">Concepts</span>
  <h2>The reference for today</h2>
  <p class="sub">Keep this open. The table answers most questions in the steps below.</p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Reservoir</th><th>Typical residence time</th><th>Fast or slow?</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><strong>Atmosphere</strong> (water vapor, clouds)</td><td>about 9 days</td><td>Fast</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Soil moisture</strong></td><td>1 to 2 months</td><td>Fast</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Rivers</strong></td><td>2 to 6 months</td><td>Fast</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Seasonal snow</strong></td><td>2 to 6 months</td><td>Fast</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Lakes</strong> (large ones)</td><td>10 to 100 years</td><td>Slow</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Mountain glaciers</strong></td><td>20 to 100 years</td><td>Slow</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Groundwater</strong></td><td>shallow: years to a few centuries · deep: up to about 10,000 years</td><td>Slow</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Ocean</strong></td><td>about 3,000 years</td><td>Slow</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Ice sheets</strong> (Antarctica, Greenland)</td><td>tens of thousands of years</td><td>Slowest</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p class="entry-meta" style="margin-top: 8px;">These are textbook averages. Real values vary a lot from place to place; the order of the rows matters more than the exact numbers.</p>
  <div class="callout warn" style="margin-top: 12px;">
    <div class="callout-title">One word, two meanings: "reservoir"</div>
    In everyday English in India, a reservoir is a lake behind a dam, like Mettur or Krishna Raja Sagar. In science, a reservoir is <strong>any place water is stored</strong>, including the air and the rock under your feet. Today we use the science meaning. A dam lake is one kind of reservoir, not the only kind.
  </div>
